    'ratios' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Use ratios to compare two quantities and describe their relationship.','essential_question'=>'How can a ratio communicate a comparison clearly?','learning_targets'=>"I can write ratios in three forms.\nI can generate equivalent ratios.\nI can interpret a ratio in context.",'vocabulary'=>"Ratio — a comparison of two quantities\nEquivalent ratios — ratios naming the same comparison\nPart-to-part — comparison of two parts\nPart-to-whole — comparison of one part to the total",'worked_examples'=>"Write a ratio | 8 red to 12 blue is 8:12. | Divide both terms by 4. | The simplified ratio is 2:3.\nUse a table | Start with 3 cups juice to 2 cups water. | Multiply both terms by 2. | 6:4 is equivalent to 3:2.",'common_mistakes'=>"Reversing the order | Match each number to the order named in the question.\nSimplifying only one term | Multiply or divide both terms by the same nonzero number.",'real_life'=>'Ratios describe recipes, maps, mixtures, teams, scale models, and comparisons in data.','videos'=>'Ratios introduction | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations/cc-6th-ratios','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Meaning of a ratio\n1:20 | Three ratio forms\n2:45 | Equivalent ratios\n4:20 | Ratio tables\n5:45 | Context interpretation",'practice_warmup'=>'Write the ratio of 6 circles to 9 squares in simplest form. | 2:3 | Keep the named order. | Divide both terms by 3. | 6:9 = 2:3.','guided_practice'=>"A class has 12 laptops for 18 students. Simplify the ratio. | 2:3 | Write 12:18. | Divide both by 6. | 2 laptops for every 3 students.\nComplete 4:7 = 12:?. | 21 | Four was multiplied by 3. | Multiply 7 by 3. | 12:21.",'independent_practice'=>"Simplify 15:25. | 3:5 | Divide by the GCF. | | 15:25 = 3:5.\nFind an equivalent ratio to 7:4 with first term 21. | 21:12 | Multiply by 3. | | 7:4 = 21:12.",'challenge_practice'=>'Design two different collections that have a 3:5 part-to-part ratio. Explain how both collections preserve the comparison.','master_it'=>"I can preserve ratio order.\nI can simplify and generate equivalent ratios.\nI can explain what both terms mean in context.",'mastery_questions'=>"Which ratio compares 8 cats to 12 dogs? | 8:12 ; 12:8 ; 8:20 ; 20:12 | A\nWhich is equivalent to 2:3? | 4:5 ; 6:9 ; 8:9 ; 10:12 | B\nSimplify 15:25. | 2:3 ; 3:5 ; 5:3 ; 6:10 | B\nWhat must happen to both ratio terms? | Add different values ; Use the same factor ; Reverse them ; Ignore units | B\nA 3:4 ratio scaled by 5 is? | 8:9 ; 15:20 ; 12:15 ; 3:20 | B",'parent_summary'=>'Students use ratios to compare quantities, create equivalent comparisons, and explain the meaning of each term.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What two quantities are being compared?\nAsk: Does order matter here?\nAsk your child to prove two ratios are equivalent.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Choose two colors of household objects, write their ratio in three forms, then create an equivalent ratio by scaling both terms.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will represent ratios in multiple forms.\nStudents will generate and simplify equivalent ratios.\nStudents will interpret part-to-part and part-to-whole comparisons.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Compare two-color collections.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Model order and equivalent ratios.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause for predictions.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Use tables and contexts.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require interpretation.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Order does not matter | Label quantities before writing the ratio.\nA ratio is subtraction | Use matched groups and multiplicative language.\nOnly one term is scaled | Trace the common scale factor.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use counters and labeled frames.\nOn Level | Mix part-to-part and part-to-whole contexts.\nAdvanced | Compare three-part ratios.\nMultilingual Learners | Use sentence frames: __ for every __.",'teacher_formative'=>"Have students build a ratio from counters.\nAsk for two equivalent ratios on mini-whiteboards.\nUse an exit ticket requiring words and symbols.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.RP.A.1 — Understand ratio concepts and use ratio language.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.RP.A.3 — Use ratio reasoning to solve problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP6 — Reason quantitatively and attend to precision."
