    'rates-unit-rates' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Compare quantities with different units and find the amount for one unit.','essential_question'=>'Why does a unit rate make different situations easier to compare?','learning_targets'=>"I can identify a rate and its units.\nI can calculate a unit rate.\nI can use unit rates to compare choices.",'vocabulary'=>"Rate — a ratio comparing different units\nUnit rate — a rate with a denominator of one\nUnit price — cost for one item or unit\nConstant speed — equal distance per unit of time",'worked_examples'=>"Find speed | 180 miles in 3 hours. | Divide both quantities by 3. | 60 miles per hour.\nFind unit price | $12 for 8 notebooks. | Compute 12 ÷ 8. | $1.50 per notebook.",'common_mistakes'=>"Dividing in the wrong order | Put the requested unit in the numerator.\nDropping units | Write and interpret units at every step.",'real_life'=>'Unit rates compare prices, speeds, wages, fuel use, recipes, and data plans.','videos'=>'Unit rates | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations/cc-6th-rates','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Rates and units\n1:15 | Dividing to one\n2:40 | Unit price\n4:00 | Speed\n5:30 | Comparing rates",'practice_warmup'=>'A car travels 120 miles in 2 hours. Find the unit rate. | 60 miles per hour | Divide distance by time. | 120 ÷ 2. | 60 mph.','guided_practice'=>"$18 for 12 bottles. Find unit price. | $1.50 per bottle | Divide cost by bottles. | 18 ÷ 12. | $1.50 each.\n240 words in 4 minutes. | 60 words per minute | Divide words by minutes. | 240 ÷ 4. | 60 wpm.",'independent_practice'=>"$9.60 for 6 pounds. | $1.60 per pound | Divide cost by pounds. | | 9.60 ÷ 6 = 1.60.\n315 miles on 9 gallons. | 35 miles per gallon | Divide miles by gallons. | | 315 ÷ 9 = 35.",'challenge_practice'=>'Create two package deals in which the larger package has the lower unit price. Show the calculations and explain the better buy.','master_it'=>"I can divide in the order required by the units.\nI can state a unit rate with labels.\nI can compare options using the same unit.",'mastery_questions'=>"180 miles in 3 hours equals? | 30 mph ; 60 mph ; 90 mph ; 540 mph | B\n$12 for 8 items equals? | $1.25 ; $1.50 ; $2 ; $4 | B\nA unit rate has denominator? | 0 ; 1 ; 10 ; 100 | B\nWhich helps compare packages? | Total only ; Unit price ; Color ; Brand length | B\n240 words in 4 minutes equals? | 40 ; 50 ; 60 ; 80 | C",'parent_summary'=>'Students divide rates to a denominator of one and use labeled unit rates to compare real choices.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What does per mean?\nAsk: Which quantity should be divided by which?\nAsk your child to compare two grocery prices.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Select two package sizes from a receipt or advertisement and calculate the cost per item for each.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will distinguish ratios from rates.\nStudents will calculate and label unit rates.\nStudents will compare situations with common units.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Compare package prices.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Model division and units.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Predict unit labels.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Mix price and speed.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Justify a choice.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students invert the quotient | Read units as a fraction before dividing.\nStudents compare totals | Convert both to the same unit rate.\nStudents omit units | Require units in every answer.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use ratio tables to scale to one.\nOn Level | Use decimals and mixed contexts.\nAdvanced | Compare multi-step rates.\nMultilingual Learners | Pair per with each and for every.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask students to annotate numerator and denominator units.\nUse a quick better-buy comparison.\nCheck whether answers include meaningful labels.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.RP.A.2 — Understand unit rate.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.RP.A.3.B — Solve unit-rate problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP4 — Reason and model."
    ],
    'constant-of-proportionality' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Identify the constant multiplicative relationship in tables, graphs, and equations.','essential_question'=>'How does one constant connect every pair of values in a proportional relationship?','learning_targets'=>"I can calculate k = y/x.\nI can identify k in a table, graph, or equation.\nI can explain what k means in context.",'vocabulary'=>"Proportional relationship — a relationship with constant y/x\nConstant of proportionality — the fixed multiplier k\nOrigin — the point (0,0)\nEquation — y = kx",'worked_examples'=>"Find k from a table | Use x=3, y=12. | Compute 12 ÷ 3. | k=4.\nFind k from y=2.5x | Compare with y=kx. | The coefficient of x is k. | k=2.5.",'common_mistakes'=>"Computing x/y | Use output divided by input: y/x.\nCalling any linear graph proportional | A proportional graph must pass through the origin.",'real_life'=>'Constants of proportionality model prices, speeds, recipes, scale drawings, and hourly earnings.','videos'=>'Constant of proportionality | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-seventh-grade-math/cc-7th-ratio-proportion','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Proportional relationships\n1:15 | k from tables\n2:45 | k from graphs\n4:10 | k from equations\n5:35 | Meaning and units",'practice_warmup'=>'If y=15 when x=3, find k. | 5 | Use k=y/x. | 15 ÷ 3. | k=5.','guided_practice'=>"Points (2,7) and (4,14). Find k. | 3.5 | Divide y by x. | 7 ÷ 2. | Both pairs give 3.5.\nFor y=0.8x, identify k. | 0.8 | Compare to y=kx. | Read the coefficient. | k=0.8.",'independent_practice'=>"Find k for x: 3,6,9 and y: 12,24,36. | 4 | Compute y/x. | | Every quotient is 4.\nDoes y=3x+2 represent a proportion? | No | Check the origin. | | The +2 means it does not pass through (0,0).",'challenge_practice'=>'Create a table and graph for a proportional relationship with a fractional constant. Prove the same k appears in every representation.','master_it'=>"I can calculate y/x accurately.\nI can verify the quotient is constant.\nI can connect k across tables, graphs, equations, and contexts.",'mastery_questions'=>"If x=4 and y=20, k=? | 4 ; 5 ; 16 ; 24 | B\nIn y=7x, k=? | x ; y ; 7 ; 0 | C\nA proportional graph passes through? | (0,0) ; (0,1) ; (1,0) ; any intercept | A\nWhich quotient finds k? | x/y ; y/x ; x+y ; y-x | B\nIs y=2x+1 proportional? | Yes ; No ; Sometimes ; Only for x=1 | B",'parent_summary'=>'Students find and interpret the fixed multiplier that connects input and output in a proportional relationship.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What stays constant?\nAsk: How do you know the graph is proportional?\nAsk your child to name the units of k.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Make a price table for identical items, divide total cost by quantity in each row, and identify the constant unit price.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will compute k from ordered pairs and tables.\nStudents will identify k in y=kx.\nStudents will distinguish proportional from nonproportional linear relationships.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Notice a repeated multiplier.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Connect four representations.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause to calculate k.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Include nonexamples.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require interpretation.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students use x/y | Label input and output before calculating.\nStudents use differences | Contrast constant additive and multiplicative change.\nStudents ignore the origin | Test x=0 in the equation.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use integer ratio tables.\nOn Level | Mix all representations.\nAdvanced | Use fractional and negative constants.\nMultilingual Learners | Use input-output-unit sentence frames.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask for k and its units from one ordered pair.\nUse representation-matching cards.\nEnd with one proportional and one nonproportional example.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.2 — Recognize and represent proportional relationships.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.2.B–D — Identify, represent, and explain k.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP4, and MP7 — Reason, model, and use structure."
    ],
    'percents' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Use percent as a rate per 100 to solve part, whole, and percent problems.','essential_question'=>'How does thinking per 100 connect fractions, decimals, and real-world percent problems?','learning_targets'=>"I can convert among fractions, decimals, and percents.\nI can find a percent of a quantity.\nI can solve percent increase and decrease problems.",'vocabulary'=>"Percent — a rate per 100\nOriginal amount — starting value\nPercent change — change divided by original amount\nDiscount — amount subtracted from price",'worked_examples'=>"Find 25% of 80 | Write 25% as 0.25. | Multiply 0.25×80. | The part is 20.\nFind percent | 18 is what percent of 60? | Compute 18÷60=0.3. | 30%.",'common_mistakes'=>"Using 25 instead of 0.25 | Divide the percent by 100 before multiplying.\nUsing the new amount as the percent-change base | Divide change by the original amount.",'real_life'=>'Percents describe discounts, taxes, tips, interest, markups, test scores, and data.','videos'=>'Percent problems | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-seventh-grade-math/cc-7th-ratio-proportion/cc-7th-percent-word-problems','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Percent means per 100\n1:20 | Convert forms\n2:45 | Find the part\n4:00 | Find the percent\n5:15 | Increase and decrease",'practice_warmup'=>'Write 35% as a decimal. | 0.35 | Divide by 100. | Move the decimal two places left. | 35%=0.35.','guided_practice'=>"Find 40% of 75. | 30 | Write 0.40×75. | Multiply. | 30.\nA $50 item is 20% off. Find sale price. | $40 | Find 20% of 50. | Subtract $10. | $40.",'independent_practice'=>"15 is what percent of 60? | 25% | Divide part by whole. | | 15÷60=0.25=25%.\nIncrease 80 by 12%. | 89.60 | Find 0.12×80. | | Add 9.60 to 80.",'challenge_practice'=>'Create a shopping receipt containing a discount, tax, and tip. Show why the order and base amount for each percent matter.','master_it'=>"I can choose the correct base or whole.\nI can convert percent to decimal before calculating.\nI can explain whether to add or subtract the percent amount.",'mastery_questions'=>"25% as a decimal is? | 0.025 ; 0.25 ; 2.5 ; 25 | B\n25% of 80 is? | 20 ; 25 ; 40 ; 55 | A\n18 is what percent of 60? | 18% ; 30% ; 42% ; 60% | B\nA 20% discount on $50 is? | $5 ; $10 ; $20 ; $40 | B\nPercent change uses which base? | New amount ; Original amount ; Difference only ; 100 always | B",'parent_summary'=>'Students connect percent to hundredths and solve real problems involving a part, whole, percent, increase, and decrease.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What is the whole or original amount?\nAsk: Should this percent be added or subtracted?\nAsk your child to estimate before calculating.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Choose a store price and mentally estimate 10%, 25%, and 50%, then calculate one realistic discount.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will convert among percent representations.\nStudents will solve part-whole-percent problems.\nStudents will model percent increase and decrease in context.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Use a hundred grid.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Connect equations and models.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Predict reasonableness.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Use consumer contexts.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require base identification.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students multiply by a whole percent | Convert to decimal or fraction first.\nStudents choose the wrong whole | Label part, whole, and percent.\nStudents subtract the percent number | Calculate the percent amount first.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use benchmark percents and grids.\nOn Level | Mix missing part, whole, and percent.\nAdvanced | Use successive percent changes.\nMultilingual Learners | Use labeled percent proportion frames.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask students to identify the whole before solving.\nUse estimate-then-calculate checks.\nSort errors by conversion, base, or operation.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.RP.A.3.C — Find a percent of a quantity.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.3 — Solve multistep percent problems.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP1, MP2, and MP4 — Persevere, reason, and model."
    ],
    'proportional-relationships' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Represent proportional relationships with equations of the form y = kx.','essential_question'=>'How does y = kx describe every pair in a proportional relationship?','learning_targets'=>"I can write y=kx from a table or context.\nI can use an equation to find missing values.\nI can interpret variables and k with units.",'vocabulary'=>"Independent variable — input quantity x\nDependent variable — output quantity y\nCoefficient — number multiplying a variable\nProportional equation — y=kx",'worked_examples'=>"Write an equation | Tickets cost $6 each. | Let x be tickets and y be cost. | y=6x.\nUse the equation | For 8 tickets, y=6(8). | Multiply. | y=48.",'common_mistakes'=>"Adding an extra constant | A proportional equation has no added term.\nSwitching variables | Define each variable and attach units before writing the equation.",'real_life'=>'Proportional equations model constant prices, speeds, wages, recipes, conversions, and scale drawings.','videos'=>'Equations for proportional relationships | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-seventh-grade-math/cc-7th-ratio-proportion','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Variables and quantities\n1:20 | Find k\n2:40 | Write y=kx\n4:00 | Use the equation\n5:20 | Interpret solutions",'practice_warmup'=>'A movie costs $9 per ticket. Write an equation for total cost y and tickets x. | y=9x | The unit price is k. | Substitute k=9. | y=9x.','guided_practice'=>"Water flows at 4 gallons per minute. | y=4x | Let x be minutes. | Let y be gallons. | y=4x.\nUse y=2.5x when x=12. | 30 | Substitute 12. | Multiply 2.5×12. | y=30.",'independent_practice'=>"Table: x=2,4,6; y=14,28,42. Write equation. | y=7x | Find y/x. | | k=7.\nDoes y=5x+3 represent a proportion? | No | Check its form and origin. | | The added 3 makes it nonproportional.",'challenge_practice'=>'Invent a real situation modeled by y=1.75x. Define both variables and units, create a table, and explain the meaning of 1.75.','master_it'=>"I can define x and y in context.\nI can find and interpret k.\nI can write, use, and verify y=kx.",'mastery_questions'=>"At $6 each, cost equation is? | y=x+6 ; y=6x ; x=6y ; y=x/6 | B\nIf y=4x and x=7, y=? | 3 ; 11 ; 28 ; 47 | C\nWhich is proportional? | y=3x ; y=3x+1 ; y=x-3 ; y=3 | A\nIn y=2.5x, k=? | x ; y ; 2.5 ; 0 | C\nA proportional equation passes through? | (0,0) ; (0,1) ; (1,0) ; (1,1) always | A",'parent_summary'=>'Students translate constant-rate situations into y=kx, use equations to predict values, and interpret every symbol with units.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What does x represent?\nAsk: What does k mean with units?\nAsk your child how the equation predicts a new value.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Choose a constant price, define quantity x and total y, write y=kx, and calculate totals for three quantities.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will write proportional equations from contexts and tables.\nStudents will evaluate equations for given inputs.\nStudents will interpret variables and coefficients with units.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Describe a constant-price pattern.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Move table to equation.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause for variable definitions.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Translate both directions.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require interpretation.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students add k instead of multiplying | Connect repeated groups to multiplication.\nStudents reverse x and y | Label input and output columns with units.\nStudents accept y=kx+b | Test whether the graph passes through the origin.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Build equations from ratio tables.\nOn Level | Mix decimal rates and contexts.\nAdvanced | Compare proportional and affine equations.\nMultilingual Learners | Use Let x represent... sentence frames.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask students to define variables before solving.\nUse equation-context matching cards.\nCheck one substitution and one written interpretation.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.2.C — Represent proportional relationships by equations.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.RP.A.2.D — Explain points on graphs.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2, MP4, and MP6 — Reason, model, and attend to precision."
    ]
